Sulfate radicals-based advanced oxidation processes are regarded as effective methods for removing organic contaminants. However, current removal technologies are often unable to achieve satisfactory results due to the low concentrations and high stability that are frequently characteristics for many types of organic contaminants. To improve the efficacy of using sulfate radicals to remove organic contaminants, we designed metal–organic frameworks (Fe(II)-MOFs) modified with enrichment and specific recognition of molecular imprinting layer (Fe (II)-MOFs@MIP). Using dibutyl phthalate (DBP) as the model target contaminant, the selectivity coefficient towards DBP was 7.28 for adsorption and 4.46 for catalytic degradation of $\mathrm{Fe(II)-MOFs}@\mathrm{MIP}$ , which is excellent for most of the imprinted material. Additionally, we achieved the accurate recognition $(169.25~\upmu\up g\times\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ and efficient degradation $(0.071\ \mathrm{min}^{-1}).$ of DBP reducing levels to $100~\upmu\mathbf{g}\times\mathbf{L}^{-1}$ . UV–vis analysis confirmed that specific recognition of DBP was mainly through hydrogen bonding and $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction, while in-situ Raman and radical quenching experiments showed that the imprinting layer could accurately adsorb and produce the spatial confinement effects on target contaminants, thereby reducing the transfer distance of free radicals. As a result, we show that $\mathrm{Fe(II){\cdot}M O F s}@\mathrm{MIP}$ can achieve efficient targeted degradation of DBP, opening up a new avenue for the removal of this and other highly stable organic contaminants present at low concentrations in environmental systems.
## 1. Introduction
Due to the extensive use of antibiotics, plastics, and personal skin care products, concentrations of organic contaminants, such as cipro­ floxacin, bisphenol A, and propyl p-hydroxybenzoate, in water have reached levels in ecosystems that can pose serious threats to both sen­ sitive habits and human health [1]. Most organic pollutants, however, cannot be easily removed due to their stable structure and propensity for bioaccumulation, generating a social problem that needs to be urgently solved. Dibutyl phthalate (DBP) as one type of common organic pol­ lutants, which frequently detected in water, soil, foods, and atmosphere [2], a chemical used in building materials, food packing, and medical devices, cosmetics and that has attracted considerable attention due to its carcinogenicity, immune toxicity as well as its effects on the endo­ crine system even at very low concentrations [3,4]. As such, DBP has been identified as an environmental priority pollutant by both the US
Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) and the Chinese State Envi­ ronmental Protection Administration (SEPA) [5]. Thus, the issue of eliminating DBP from the aquatic environment must be urgently addressed.
Various traditional methods of organic pollutant removal include adsorption [6], biodegradation [7], and advanced oxidation processes (AOPs) [8]. Among them, AOPs showed the most promise for DBP degradation due to their simplicity, rapidity and high efficiency [9]. Notably, sulfate radical-based AOPs have received extensive attention because $s0_{4}^{-\bullet}$ possesses a number of inherent merits, such as a relatively high redox potential (2.5–3.1 V), broadly applicable pH range (2.0–8.0), and strong reaction selectivity [10,11]. Additionally, $s0_{4}^{-\bullet}$ prefers singleelectron transfer reactions with low toxicity of $s0_{4}^{-\bullet}$ -addition in­ termediates, making it more suitable for degrading DBP [12]. Thus, the activation of persulfate (PS) to generate reactive radicals by transition metals heterogeneous catalysts with low energy consumption has been extensively studied [13]. The short radical lifetimes of PS hamper the effective diffusion of reactive radicals into the contaminants in waste­ water, however, previously tested AOPs have not been shown to effec­ tively degrade low concentration contaminants in wastewater containing various pollutants [14]. Therefore, novel heterogeneous catalysts that can effectively activate PS and degrade the low concen­ tration pollutants are urgently needed.
Metal-organic frameworks (MOFs), as novel materials built from metal ions (or clusters) and organic ligand, have drawn increasing attention as potential candidate catalysts due to their tunable porosity, high surface areas, and high catalytic activity [15,16]. Furthermore, ferrous MOFs have unsaturated metal sites, non-toxicity, and easy availability of iron atoms in crustal minerals, making them excellent heterogeneous activators of PS [17,18]. Though the selectivity of ferrous MOFs is currently too poor to specifically remove low concentration contaminants, the numerous carboxyl groups in their structure provide the possibility to design the more reasonable catalysts that allow for easy and precise modification with other functional materials [19]. In recent years, using molecular imprinting technology to modify MOFs has gradually attracted the attention of researchers. Molecularly imprinted polymers (MIPs) are designed to create tailor-made molecular recogni­ tion sites that match well with the shapes, sizes, and functional groups of the template molecules, thereby achieving selective binding and recognition [20]. The feasibility of selective removal of target contam­ inants by MIPs combined with other environmental functional materials has been demonstrated. For example, it has been shown that molecularly imprinted decorated specific facets of single $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ performed well in degrading dimethyl phthalate [21,22]. Previous studies also show that MIPs can achieve good selectivity for DBP via the formation of specific recognition sites [23]. Few studies, however, have focused on the increasing the recognition and degradation capacity of MIPs modified with MOFs within applications for heterogeneous activators of PS [24]. Moreover, the mechanisms for specific recognition and the synergetic effects of specific recognition and catalytic degradation of MIPs modi­ fied with Fe(II)-MOFs heterogeneous materials are still not clear.
In this study, we demonstrate the selective adsorption and catalytic capacity of a novel molecularly imprinted Fe(II)-MOFs (Fe(II)-MOFs $@$ ­ MIP) was synthesized. The selective adsorption and catalytic capacity of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP were studied to investigate its degradation perfor­ mance towards DBP and competitive contaminants. Furthermore, the mechanisms for specific recognition and catalytic degradation on DBP were also explored. It was found that MIP increased the target contaminant DBP near Fe(II)-MOFs through selective recognition. Simultaneously, multifarious coordinatively unsaturated metal sites of Fe(II)-MOFs can effectively activate PS to generate radicals, wherefore as-activated radicals achieve in-situ degradation that significantly decreased the diffusion distance between radicals and DBP.
## 2. Experimental details
## 2.1. Material
All chemicals used in this study were of analytical grade and used without further purification. DBP $(\mathsf{C}_{16}\mathsf{H}_{22}\mathsf{O}_{4},99.5\%)$ , diethyl phthalate (DEP, $\mathbf{C}_{12}\mathbf{H}_{14}\mathbf{O}_{4},99.5\%)$ , dimethyl phthalate (DMP, ${\mathrm{C}}_{10}{\mathrm{H}}_{10}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ , $99.5\%\AA$ , Dioctyl phthalate (DNOP, $\mathrm{C_{24}H_{38}O_{4}}$ , $99.5\%$ ), p-phthalic acid (1, 4-BDC, ${\mathrm{C}}_{8}{\mathrm{H}}_{6}{\mathrm{O}}_{4}$ , $99.0\%$ ), MAA $\mathrm{(C_{4}H_{6}O_{2}}$ , $99.0\%$ , azobisisobutyronitrile (AIBN, $\mathrm{C_{8}H_{12}N_{4}}$ , $99.0\%$ ) and ethylene glycol dimethacrylate (EGDMA, $\mathrm{C_{10}H_{14}O_{4}}$ , $99.0\%\right)$ were purchased from Aladdin Chemistry (Shanghai, China). Ferrous chloride $\mathrm{(FeCl_{2}{\cdot}4H_{2}O}$ , $99.0\%$ ), N, N-dimethylformamide (DMF, $\mathrm{HCO}(\mathrm{CH}_{3})_{2}$ , $99.5\%$ ), and persulfate (PS, $\mathrm{Na_{2}S_{2}O_{8}}$ , $98.0\%$ ) were supplied by Sinopharm Chemical Reagent Co., Ltd (Beijing, China). Methyl alcohol (MeOH, $\mathrm{CH}_{4}0$ , $99.5\%$ ), acetonitrile $(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{3}N},99\%)$ , and ethanol $(\mathrm{C_{2}H_{6}O},99.7\%)$ were obtained from Guangzhou Chemical Re­ agent (Guangzhou, China). Ultrapure water produced by the Millipore Mill-Q system was used throughout the experiments.
## 2.2. Preparation of MIP and non-imprinted polymer (NIP)
The precursor Fe(II)-MOFs was synthesized according to the methods detailed in our previous study with slight modifications [25]. Briefly, $1.0\mathrm{~g~}$ Fe(II)-MOFs was dispersed in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetonitrile with ultra­ sonication for $30\mathrm{min}$ (marked A), 1.25 mmol DBP and 0.75 mmol MAA were then ultrasonically dissolved in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ of acetonitrile for $30~\mathrm{{min}}$ under ${\bf N}_{2}$ atmosphere (marked B). A and $10\mathrm{mmol}$ EGDMA were added to B, and the mixture was located at $4^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ refrigerator to pre-assembled for $60\mathrm{min}$ . Afterward, $20\mathrm{mg}$ AIBN was added to the mixture with magnetic stirring for $30\ \mathrm{min}$ . The obtained solution was inflated with ${\bf N}_{2}$ gas for $20~\mathrm{{min}}$ to remove $\ensuremath{\mathbf{O}}_{2}$ and then stirred at $70\ ^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for another $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ . The resultant product was collected by filtration and washed with methanol. Finally, the material was eluted with methanol/acetic acid (9:1, $\mathbf{v}/\mathbf{v})$ several times until no template molecule could be detected. The target product was marked as Fe(II)-MOFs ${\ @\mathrm{MIP}}$ and washed with ethanol to remove the remaining acetic acid, and the clean product was dried for $^{12\mathrm{~h~}}$ at $60~^{\circ}\mathsf{C}$ under vacuum. NIP was obtained following the same procedures with the exception that DBP was not used.
## 2.3. Characterization of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIPs
The morphology of synthesized Fe(II)-MOFs@MIPs was observed by a field emission scanning electron microscope (FESEM, ZEISS Merlin, Germany) coupled with an energy-dispersive X-ray spectroscopy (EDS). Powder X-ray diffraction (PXRD) measurements were obtained using a Bruker D8 diffract meter equipped with Cu Kα X-rays. Transmission electron microscopy (TEM) was carried out with a JEM-2100 instrument operated at $200\ensuremath{\mathrm{kV}}$ and the distribution of the elements of the samples were analyzed by energy-dispersive X-ray spectrometer (TEM-EDX). Fourier transform infrared spectra (FT-IR) were recorded on a Nicolet Magna 550 FT-IR spectrometer. Specific surface area and pore size dis­ tribution were measured at $77~\mathrm{K}$ on an ASAP 2020 analyzer (Micro­ meritics, USA). The electron paramagnetic resonance spectrum (EPR, Bruker EMX 10/12 spectrometer) was used to identify the involved reactive radical species. The specific structural information about the DBP degradation process was obtained from the in-situ Raman spectrum (Renishaw InVia).
## 2.4. Adsorption and degradation experiments
To measure in-batch adsorption experiments, $100~\mathrm{{\mg}~\ F e(I I)}$ - MOFs@MIPs or Fe(II)-MOFs@NIPs was placed into $100~\mathrm{{mL}}$ of DBP so­ lution $(60~\mathrm{mg/mL})$ and shaken with $180~\mathrm{rpm}$ at room temperature for different time intervals until adsorption equilibrium. Samples were filtered with a $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ water phase filter and collected for the deter­ mination of the residual filtered DBP concentrations.
To measure degradation, $70~\mathrm{mg~PS}$ (PS: $\mathsf{D B P}=40{:}1\mathrm{~M~}$ ratio) was added into the above adsorption equilibrium solution with shaking for some time. At scheduled time, reactive radicals’ species of the samples were quenched by an equal amount of methanol and then filtered with $0.22~{\upmu\mathrm{m}}$ water phase filter, followed by determination of the DBP su­ pernatant concentration.
Additionally, to evaluate the mineralization efficiency of DBP during degradation experiments, total organic carbon (TOC) of the solution was determined with a TOC analyzer (HACH, BIOTECTOR B7000). Mean­ while, to evaluate the utilization of active radicals during degradation experiments, the concentration of PS was analyzed by the UV–vis spectrophotometer (Evolution 201, Thermo Fisher Scientific) at the wavelength of $352\mathrm{nm}$ .
## 2.5. Determination of DBP
The concentrations of DBP were determined with a Shimadzu LC20AT equipped with an Agilent Hypersil C18 column $250\ \mathrm{mm}\times4.6$ $\mathbf{mm}$ , i.d., $5\upmu\mathrm{m}$ particle size) linked to a variable wavelength detector at
$\lambda=228\mathrm{nm}$ . A mixture of methanol and water at 90:10 (v/v) ratios were used as the mobile phase with a constant flow rate of $1.0\ \mathrm{mL/min}$ and sample injection volume of $10~\upmu\mathrm{L}$ at $30~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ .
## 2.6. Selectivity adsorption and degradation study
To evaluate the specificity of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP and $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ - $\mathsf{M O F s@N I P}$ , diethyl phthalate (DEP), dimethyl phthalate (DMP), and dinoctyl phthalate (DNOP) (Fig. S1) were used as interferons with the initial concentration of $60~\mathrm{{mg/L}}$ . Both selective adsorption and degra­ dation experiments were run for $120\mathrm{min}$ . The mixtures including DEP, DMP, and DNOP were detected by HPLC, using similar procedures to those described above for measuring adsorption and degradation.
The pollutant adsorption selectivity coefficient $(k_{\alpha})$ and degradation selectivity coefficient $(k_{\beta})$ were calculated as follows [26].
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{k_{\alpha}=\frac{a d s o r p t i o n c a p a b i l i t y(t e m p l a t e)}{a d s o r p t i o n c a p a b i l i t y(c o m p e t i t i o n s)}}}\\ {{}}\\ {{k_{\beta}=\frac{d e g r a d a t i o n c a p a b i l i t y(t e m p l a t e)}{d e g r a d a t i o n c a p a b i l i t y(c o m p e t i t i o n s)}}}\end{array}
$$
The material adsorption selectivity coefficient $(k_{\alpha}^{\prime})$ and degradation selectivity coefficient $(\boldsymbol{k}_{\beta}^{\prime})$ were calculated using the following equations [27]:
$$
\begin{array}{r}{k^{'}_{\alpha}=\frac{k_{\alpha}\left(i m p r i n t e d\mathrm{polymer}\right)}{k_{\alpha}\left(n o n-i m p r i n t e d\mathrm{polymer}\right)}}\\ {k^{'}_{\beta}=\frac{k_{\beta}\left(i m p r i n t e d\mathrm{polymer}\right)}{k_{\beta}\left(n o n-i m p r i n t e d\mathrm{polymer}\right)}}\end{array}
$$
## 2.7. In-situ Raman spectrum experiments
The degradation of DBP on Fe(II)-MOFs and Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP electrodes was performed in an H-type in-situ Raman electrolysis cell filled with 0.1 M $\mathrm{KHCO}_{3}$ electrolyte that containing DBP and PS. Meanwhile, the in-situ Raman was applied to probe the degradation process. The wavelength of the excitation laser was $785\mathrm{nm}$ (the power was about $10\mathrm{mW},$ . The Raman shift was calibrated to $750\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ using a Si wafer. Each Raman spectrum displayed here was recorded with six accumulations over an acquisition time of $10s$ .
## 3. Results and discussion
## 3.1. Optimal conditions and characterization for prepared Fe(II)- MOFs@MIP
Concentrations of DBP, MAA, and EGDMA play a key role in mo­ lecular imprinting. Thus, a response surface model (RSM) coupled with a central composite design (CCD) was performed to optimize the con­ centrations of DBP, MAA, and EGDMA. (Table S1). Optimum conditions under which Fe(II)-MOFs $@\mathrm{MIP}$ exhibited superior degradation effi­ ciency for DBP were $1.25~\mathrm{mmol}$ DBP, 0.75 mmol MAA and 10 mmol EGDMA $(\mathrm{R}^{2}=0.9211$ ; $\mathbf{p}<0.05\dot{}$ ) (Fig. S2).
The morphology and structure of the obtained Fe(II)-MOFs, Fe(II)- $\mathbf{MOFs@MIP}$ , and Fe(II)-MOFs@NIP were observed by FESEM. Fe(II)- MOFs exhibited a rod structure with a smooth surface before molecu­ lar imprinting (Panel A), while the surface of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP (Panel B) and $\mathrm{Fe(II)-MOFs}@\mathrm{MIP}$ (Panel C) became rough along with many irregular deposits, which are believed to be the imprinted polymers (Fig. 1). Meanwhile, the imprinted polymer for Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP was looser than the polymer for Fe(II)-MOFs@NIP due to the formation of the relative cavities of the temple DBP in the imprinted layer, which was beneficial for enhancing the ability to adsorb DBP.
The microstructure of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP was also investigated by TEM. The MIP layer was dispersed homogeneously on the surface of Fe (II)-MOFs (the dark areas in Fig. 1D), while the thickness of the MIP layer deposited on the surface of Fe(II)-MOFs was about $200{-}300\mathrm{nm}$ . To further verify the presence of the MIP layer in Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP, highangle annular dark-field (HAADF) and corresponding energy-dispersive spectrometry (EDS) mappings of Fe, C, and O elements were generated (Fig. 1E). Obviously, Fe(II)-MOFs was distributed in the innermost part of the sector areas, while the outer layer was covered with the MIP layer from HAADF. Additionally, Fe element occupied a small part of the sector, but C and O elements were distributed throughout the sector. This is because Fe element is only derived from Fe(II)-MOFs, while C and O elements exist in both Fe(II)-MOFs and MIP layer.
The crystal structure of the materials was analyzed using PXRD (Fig. 2A). The XRD patterns of both Fe(II)-MOFs ${\ @\mathrm{MIP}}$ and $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ - $\mathsf{M O F s@N I P}$ exhibited the main characteristic diffraction peaks of Fe (II)-MOFs at $2\uptheta\:=7.5^{\circ}$ , $9.2^{\circ}$ , $10.5^{\circ}$ and $22.1^{\circ}$ [25]. Though the in­ tensities of the peak at $2\theta=10.5^{\circ}$ for $\mathrm{Fe(II)-MOFs}@\mathrm{MIP}$ and Fe(II)- $\scriptstyle\mathbf{MOFs@NIP}$ decreased significantly, other peaks only exhibited tiny differences in normalized intensity, indicating that the molecular imprinting procedure did not significantly alter the crystalline and structural integrity of their matrices.
FT-IR was carried out to identify the characteristic functional groups on the surface of the obtained materials (Fig. 2B). The spectra of Fe(II)- $\mathbf{MOFs@MIP}$ and $\mathrm{Fe(II)-MOFs}@\mathrm{NIP}$ still retained many characteristic absorption peaks of $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ -MOFs, specifically at 1528 and $1386~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (carbonyl $-\mathsf{C}=0$ and -C-O stretching vibrations) [28] and at $752\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ (C–H bending vibration in the aromatic ring) [29], indicating the pres­ ence of organic ligand terephthalic acid in Fe(II)-MOFs. Additionally, an absorption band at $752~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ was associated with the stretching vibra­ tion of Fe-O [30], indicating a coordination bond between metal central ion and an organic ligand. Compared with other Fe(II)-MOFs, however, two new characteristic absorption peaks at $1162~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ and $707~\mathrm{cm^{-}}$ 1were observed, corresponding to the stretching vibrations of tert-butyl from MAA and EGDMA[31] and the $=\mathrm{C-H}$ bending vibrations of the polymerization between EGDMA and benzene ring[32], respectively suggesting that the MIP layer was grafted onto the surface of Fe(II)- MOFs. No significant difference was observed in the characteristic peaks of Fe(II)-MOFs@NIP and Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP in the range of $400{-}2000~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ indicating that the template DBP had been removed from the imprinted polymers.
The $\mathbf{N}_{2}$ adsorption–desorption isotherm and corresponding pore size distribution plots were studied using the DFT method for obtained ma­ terials. The respective specific surface area and pore volume of Fe(II)- MOFs were $534.7\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $0.54~\mathrm{m}^{3}/\mathrm{g}$ (Table S2 and Fig. 2C). Howev­ er, the $\mathrm{Fe(II)-MOFs}@\mathrm{NIP}$ and $\mathrm{Fe(II)-MOFs}@\mathrm{MIP}$ exhibited a smaller specific surface area of $\mathrm{Fe(II)}$ -MOFs (the specific surface area of Fe(II)- $\mathsf{M O F s@N I P}$ and Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP were $342.1\ \mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ and $362.3~\mathrm{m}^{2}/\mathrm{g}$ , the pore volume of Fe(II)-MOFs@NIP and Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP were 0.39 $\mathbf{m}^{3}/\bar{\mathbf{g}}$ and $0.41\mathrm{\m}^{3}/\mathrm{g})$ indicating that the specific surface area of Fe(II)- MOFs might be partially blocked by the imprinted polymer layer. The DFT pore size curves in Fig. 2D showed that Fe(II)-MOFs ${\boldsymbol{\ @}}\mathbf{MIP}$ had four characteristic pore structures of $1.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $2.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ , $2.7~\mathrm{nm}$ , and $3.5~\mathrm{{nm}}$ , respectively. The pore size distribution centered at $2.2\ \mathrm{nm}$ and $2.7\ \mathrm{nm}$ agrees with two characteristic channels of Fe(II)-MOFs observed in previous studies [33]. The pore size distribution centered at $1.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ agrees with the characteristic channels of Fe(II)-MOFs@NIP observed, which was attributed to the imprinted layer formed by the polymeri­ zation of MAA and EGDMA. While pore size distribution at $3.5\mathrm{nm}$ was likely due to the removal of the template DBP after molecular imprinting [34].
## 3.2. Adsorption kinetic study
The DBP adsorption kinetic was studied by determining the variation of the DBP absorption capacity from 0 to $180~\mathrm{{min}}$ with an initial con­ centration of $40\mathrm{\bar{mg}L^{-1}}$ . The DBP adsorption on Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP oc­ curs rapidly during the first $60\mathrm{min}$ , followed by a slow increase between $60~\mathrm{{min}}$ and $120~\mathrm{min}$ , and then gradually reached a plateau (Fig. 3A). Similar DBP absorption trends were observed for both Fe(II)-MOFs and Fe(II)-MOFs $@\mathrm{NIP}$ . However, Fe(II)-MOFs $@\mathrm{MIP}$ displayed much higher adsorption capacity $(65.03\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}\cdot$ ) than those of Fe(II)-MOFs (16.08 $\mathbf{mg\8}^{-1}\mathbf{\dot{\xi}}$ )and Fe(II)-MOFs@NIP $(28.83~\mathrm{mg}~\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ under the same condi­ tions, suggesting that the specific imprinting recognition sites on Fe(II)- $\mathbf{MOFs@MIP}$ surface were matched well with the sizes and spatial arrangement of the DBP template.
The pseudo-first-order model (Eq. (5)), pseudo-second-order model (Eq. (6)) and Elovich model (Eq. (7)) [35] were employed to determine the potential mechanism of adsorption.
$$
\begin{array}{l}{{q_{t}=q_{e}\left(1-\frac{1}{{e^{k_{1}t}}}\right)}}\\ {{\ }}\\ {{q_{t}=\frac{q_{e}t}{\displaystyle\frac{1}{k_{2}q_{e}}+t}~}}\end{array}
$$
$$
q_{t}=k_{p}t^{1/2}+c
$$
The pseudo-second-order model indicated a higher determination coefficient $R^{2}$ (0.998) than the pseudo-first-order (0.965) with a calcu­ lated $Q_{e}$ more consistent with the experimental value (Fig. 3B-3D and Table S3), suggesting that the pseudo-second-order model was more suitable to describe the adsorption process of DBP and that chemical adsorption was the main control process. The Elovich model (Fig. 3D) shows that the DBP adsorption process can be mainly divided into sur­ face recognition and internal pore diffusion. Meanwhile, surface recognition was the rate-controlling process rather than the internal pore diffusion.
To further investigate the absorption mechanism, the Langmuir and Freundlich isotherm models were used to interpret the movement of DBP adsorption [36]. The model equations expressed as eqs (8) and (9),
respectively:
$$
Q_{e}=\frac{K_{L}Q_{m}C_{e}}{1+K_{L}C_{e}}
$$
$$
l o g Q_{e}=\left(\frac{1}{n}\right)l o g C_{e}+l o g K_{F}
$$
where $C_{e}(\mathrm{mgL}^{-1})$ ) present the equilibrium concentration of DBP, $Q_{e}$ $(\mathrm{m}\mathrm{g}\mathrm{g}^{-1})$ and $Q_{m}(\mathbf{m}\mathbf{g}\mathbf{\Lambda}\mathbf{g}^{-1})$ are the respective equilibrium adsorption and maximum adsorption capacity of adsorbents, $K_{L}\left(\mathrm{L}/\mathrm{mg}\right)$ and $K_{F}(\mathbf{mgg^{-1}})$ are the Langmuir and Freundlich constant, respectively, and $1/n$ rep­ resents the heterogeneity index.
The Freundlich model better describes the adsorption mechanism than the Langmuir isotherm model (Table 1), suggesting that the adsorption process occurs at specific heterogeneous sites within the adsorbent and formed multilayer adsorption of DBP onto the surface of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP. Furthermore, there was a positive correlation be­ tween $K_{F}$ and the adsorption capacity of absorbents in the Freundlich isotherm model (stats). The log $K_{F}$ value of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP (2.34) was higher than that of Fe(II)-MOFs (1.15) and Fe(II)-MOFs $@\mathrm{NIP}$ (1.68), indicating that Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP exhibits the greatest affinity for the template DBP. The Freundlich constants $(\boldsymbol{1}/\boldsymbol{n})$ were between 0.386 and 0.631, signifying that the binding sites were heterogeneously distributed on the surface of the adsorbents [26]. Therefore, DBP adsorption onto Fe (II)-MOFs@MIP could belong to multilayer adsorption over a hetero­ geneous surface with non-uniform adsorption sites.
## 3.3. Catalytic performance of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP
To evaluate the catalytic performance of obtained materials, degra­ dation of DBP was measured after adsorption for $24\mathrm{~h~}$ , followed by the addition of PS into the reaction system. The adsorption capacity of Fe (II)-MOFs@MIP towards DBP was greatly improved due to the pres­ ence of imprinted polymer (Fig. 4A). Meanwhile, the degradation per­ formance of DBP by Fe(II)-MOFs ${\boldsymbol{\ @}}\mathbf{MIP}$ $(97.1\%)$ ) was significantly better than that of Fe(II)-MOFs $(27.3\%)$ ) and Fe(II)-MOFs $@\mathrm{NIP}$ $(45.6\%)$ . These results indicated that holes on the surface of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP matched with the target molecule of the DBP, resulting in an enhanced synergy between adsorption and catalysis for DBP. The excellent DBP adsorption and degradation by Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP demonstrated the great advan­ tage of the alliance between molecular imprinting and Fe(II)-MOFs, which indeed improved the adsorption and degradation capacity.
