## 3.4. Selectivity and accumulation performance study
To investigate the selectivity of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP towards DBP, the structural analogues of DBP, including DEP, DMP, and DNOP were selected as competitive contaminants in a multiple mixture system. The obtained materials had a certain adsorption capacity for the four pol­ lutants (Fig. S3A). Specifically, Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP had a high selective adsorption capacity for DBP $\left[68.86\mathrm{~mg~g^{-1}}\right]$ in the mixed system. Further, Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP demonstrated an excellent catalytic perfor­ mance for DBP $(79.06~\mathrm{mg~g^{-1}})$ in a single catalysis system, meanwhile the total removal performance reached to 139. $92\mathrm{mg}\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ (Fig. S3B). The results demonstrated that the improvement of the total removal rate was synergized by catalysis and adsorption. No significant difference in adsorption capacity towards DBP, DEP, DMP, and DNOP was observed for Fe(II)-MOFs@NIP without DBP temple (Fig. 5A and Table 2). The $k_{\alpha}$ of Fe(II)-MOFs@NIP for DBP/DEP, DBP/DMP, and DBP/DNOP was calculated to be 1.26, 1.31, and 1.37, respectively. For Fe(II)- $\mathbf{MOFs@MIP}$ with molecular imprinting, however, the absorption ca­ pacity towards DBP was significantly enhanced and showed little sensitivity to non-template molecules (DEP, DMP, and DNOP). The $k_{\alpha}$ of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP for DBP/DEP, DBP/DMP, and DBP/DNOP signifi­ cantly increased to 3.43, 5.27 and 7.28, respectively. Meanwhile, the value of $k_{\alpha}^{\prime}$ between Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP and Fe(II)-MOFs@NIP for DBP/ DNOP rose to 5.31, a value which was much higher than most of the imprinted materials in the literature [20,27,37-40]. These results confirmed that Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP exhibited excellent selectivity to­ wards the target molecule DBP. Thus, it can be concluded that Fe(II)- $\mathbf{MOFs@MIP}$ presented abundant recognition cavities with a stronger chemical and structural affinity towards DBP instead of other molecules.
As with the adsorption experiment, the degradation rate constants of Fe (II)-MOFs@NIP towards DBP, DEP, DMP, and DNOP were not significantly different with $k_{\beta}$ values for DBP/DEP (1.67), DBP/DMP (1.45), and DBP/DNOP (1.36) (Fig. 5B). In the case of Fe(II)- $\mathbf{MOFs@MIP}$ , however, the values of $k_{\beta}$ increased significantly 3.34, and 4.46 for DBP/DEP (2.57), DBP/DMP (3.34), and DBP/DNOP (4.46), proving that Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP possessed a stronger catalytic degrada­ tion selectivity to the target molecule DBP. Furthermore, $k_{\beta}^{\prime}$ between Fe (II)-MOFs@MIP and Fe (II)-MOFs $@\mathrm{NIP}$ for DBP/DNOP was 3.28, higher than most of the imprinted materials tested in previous studies (Table 2). The Fe (II)- $\mathbf{\nabla}\cdot\mathbf{MOFs@MIP}$ exhibited superior selectivity for DBP removal due to the synergy of molecular imprinting and Fe (II)-MOFs, and thus enhanced both adsorption and degradation capacity to target molecule DBP.
To further test the accumulation performance of Fe (II)-MOFs@MIP towards target contaminants at low concentrations, four sets of ultralow concentration DBP solutions (100, 200, 300, and $500~{\upmu\mathrm{g/L}})$ were conducted. The adsorption capacity and the degradation rate of Fe (II)- $\mathbf{MOFs@MIP}$ towards DBP were higher than those of Fe(II)-MOFs and Fe (II)-MOFs@MIP at DBP concentrations ranging from 100 to $500~{\upmu\mathrm{g/L}}$ (Fig. 5C and Fig. 5D). The adsorption capacity and degradation rate of Fe (II)-MOFs $@\mathrm{MIP}$ towards DBP reached t $\mathbf{01{\dot{6}9.25}\mu g\bar{~}x\textbf{g}^{-1}}$ and 0.071 $\mathrm{min}^{-1}$ even at DBP concentration of $100~\upmu\mathrm{g/L}$ . The superior selective DBP adsorption and degradation of Fe (II)-MOFs@MIP further testified the great advantage of alliance between MIP layer and with abundant coordinatively unsaturated metal sites of Fe (II)-MOFs, which enhanced both selectivity of both adsorption and degradation, and thus achieved the accurate recognition and efficient degradation of contaminants at a low level.
The effect of pH was investigated as displayed in Fig. S4A. The results were similar to most previous studies on the degradation of pollutants by MOFs activation of PS. Specifically, the pH value of the solution had a negligible effect on the activity of the obtained catalysts under neutral and acidic conditions [10,17,24,25], but the activity was greatly restricted in alkaline conditions due to most of active spices would be scavenged. Additionally, the mineralization efficiencies in the degra­ dation of DBP by the obtained materials were also measured using a TOC analyzer. The TOC removal of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP reached $58.1\%$ and was higher than both Fe(II)-MOFs $(46.7\%)$ and Fe(II)-MOFs $@\mathrm{NIP}$ $(35.1\%)$ (Fig. S4B), indicating that DBP was decomposed into small molecule substances and that the imprinted layer significantly enhanced the degradation efficiency of DBP. The stability of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP was tested by recycling experiments. The catalytic activity of Fe(II)- $\mathbf{MOFs@MIP}$ demonstrated a slightly declined within 5 cycles (Fig. S4C), which was caused by the waste of catalyst during reaction. After 5 cycles, the concentration of total iron in the reaction system was further given (Fig. S4D). The results displayed that the imprinted layer significantly reduced the dissolution of Fe in the Fe(II)-MOFs system, contributing to the imprinted layer effectively prevented $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ from destroying the Fe(II)-MOFs structure.
## 3.5. Mechanism of selective adsorption and degradation
Additionally, FT-IR spectroscopy was used to study the changes of the chemical functional groups of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP before and after recognition and degradation of DBP. The characteristic peak for Fe(II)- MOFs@MIP appeared at 1721 $\mathsf{c m}^{-1}$ representing aromatic rings (Fig. S5), but disappeared after the catalytic degradation process, indi­ cating the aromatic ring structure of DBP was broken in catalytic degradation process [27]. Therefore, our prepared Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP possessed excellent selective recognition capacity towards DBP.
To prove the target accumulation of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP to template molecule, in-situ Raman spectroscopy was employed to monitor the DBP degradation process. The characteristic peaks of DBP were observed at 1034 and $1728~\mathrm{{cm}~^{-1}}$ in addition to peaks at 811 and $1001~\mathrm{cm}^{-1}$ cor­ responding to Fe(II)-MOFs (Fig. 6B). Only the characteristic peaks of Fe (II)-MOFs were observed at $0~\mathrm{min}$ of degradation reaction. With increasing reaction time, the intensities of the DBP characteristic peaks gradually increased, ceasing around $40~\mathrm{\min}$ . Characteristic peak intensities then decreased slowly between $50\ \mathrm{min}$ and $60\ \mathrm{min}$ , until disappearing at $60\mathrm{min}$ , confirming that DBP is degraded in this process. Thus, it is verified that the removal of DBP from the obtained Fe(II)- $\mathbf{MOFs@MIP}$ is accomplished by selective adsorption and catalytic degradation.
Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP performed a good removal on DBP due to the effective activation of PS to generate strong oxidizing free radicals. To directly clarify the involved radicals, ESR analysis with DMPO as a trapping agent was performed (Fig. 6C). The characteristic signal of four split lines (symmetry center: $\begin{array}{r}{g=2.005\$ , $\mathsf{a}_{\mathrm{N}}=14.9\:\mathrm{G}$ , $\mathsf{a}_{\mathrm{H}}=14.9\mathsf{G})$ with an intensity ratio of 1:2:2:1 belonged to DMPO-•OH product, which can be attributed to OH radical. Additionally, the signal with hyperfine splitting constants of six split lines (symmetry center: $g=2.005$ , ${\bf a}_{\mathrm{N}}={\bf\Phi}$ 13.25 G, $\mathsf{a}_{\mathrm{H}}=9.97\:\mathrm{G}$ , $a_{N}^{r1}=1.25\mathrm{G}$ , $a_{H}^{r1}{=}0.82$ G) with an intensity ratio of 1:1:1:1:1:1 was ascribed to DMPO-SO•4 adduct PS activation [43]. The above results demonstrated that the existence of OH and $s0_{4}^{\bullet-}$ were verified in Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP/PS system. To explore the existence and contributions of active radicals in DBP degradation process of Fe(II)- $\mathbf{MOFs@MIP/PS}$ system, radical quenching was measured using TBA and MeOH as quenchers for OH [44]. TBA had no significant effect on DBP degradation (Fig. S6) with a degradation efficiency of $93.8\%$ still achieved in the presence of excessive TBA. In contrast, an obvious in­ hibition effect was observed after adding $100~\mathrm{{mM}}$ MeOH, the degrada­ tion efficiency of DBP was only $48.1\%$ . These results confirmed that $s0_{4}^{\bullet\cdot}$ was mainly responsible for the DBP degradation in Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP/ PS system.
To further understand the spatial confinement effects of the imprinted layer on DBP, the radical quenching experiments were pre­ formed again this time with MeOH as a scavenger. Inhibition effects on DBP degradation were observed both in Fe(II)-MOFs /PS and Fe(II)- $\mathrm{MOF}s@\mathrm{NIP/PS}$ systems, with smaller effects in Fe (II)-MOFs@MIP/PS system (Fig. 6D). DBP removal of the Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP/PS system $(48.1\%)$ was higher than both Fe(II)-MOFs/PS $(24.7\%)$ and Fe(II)- $\mathbf{MOFs@NIP/PS}$ $(28.8\%)$ . These results proved that the selective ab­ sorption and spatial confinement effects of the imprinting layer for Fe (II)-MOFs@MIP on the target molecule DBP significantly increased the utilization of $s0_{4}^{\bullet}$ , and thus improved the degradation efficiency of DBP. Besides, the consumption of PS in Fig. S7, also confirmed this conclusion.
Based on the above discussion, a reasonable mechanism for the se­ lective catalytic degradation of DBP by Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP was proposed (Scheme 1). First, selective adsorption process: The imprinted layer of Fe (II)-MOFs@MIP through hydrogen bonding and $\pi{-}\pi$ interaction can specifically adsorb the target molecule DBP rather than other pollutants with similar structure, like DEP, DMP, and DNOP in this study. This process not only increases the content of DBP on the Fe(II)-MOFs ${\ @\mathrm{MIP}}$ , but also admits PS to transfer to near the material. Simultaneously, Fe(II) active sites of $\mathrm{Fe(II)-MOFs}@\mathrm{MIP}$ could effectively activate PS to gen­ erate•OH (Eq. (10)) and $s0_{4}^{\bullet}$ (Eq. (11)), wherefore as-activated•OH and $s0_{4}^{\bullet-}$ in close vicinity to DBP have more chances to attack the target contaminant. In other words, the active radicals’ generation and DBP degradation occurred simultaneously near Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP. There­ fore, the formed active radicals can decompose the DBP rapidly, and further mineralized to $\mathsf{C O}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ over Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP (Eqs. (12, 13)).
