## 4. Conclusions
Our studies shows that Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP exhibit superior selective recognition and strong synergy of adsorption-catalytic degradation with respect to DBP removal. Compared to interferent DNOP, the selectivity coefficient towards DBP was 7.28 for adsorption and 4.46 for catalytic degradation of Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP, which is excellent for most of the imprinted materials. This relatively good performance is likely the result of the strong synergy between abundant imprinted cavities for specific recognition and active radicals’ degradation. UV–vis and FT-IR analysis showed that Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP, through hydrogen bonding and π-π interaction, achieved specific recognition of DBP. Furthermore, in-situ Raman analysis showed that the imprinting layer enabled accurate adsorption and confined DBP in close vicinity to the surface of Fe(II)- $\mathbf{MOFs@MIP}$ , and significantly decreased the diffusion distance be­ tween active radicals and DBP. Additionally, radical quenching tests and ESR analysis confirmed that $s0_{4}^{\bullet}$ was mainly responsible for the DBP degradation in Fe(II)-MOFs@MIP/PS system, also reduced the waste of $s\mathrm{O}_{4}^{\bullet}$ .
